"REMem: Reasoning with Episodic Memory in Language Agents (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.13530v1)\nciteturn31897view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2602.13530v1\",\"lineno\":65}); Total lines: 626\nL0: ##### Report GitHub Issue\nL1: \nL2: [Button: ×]\nL3: \nL4: Title: [Input: Enter title]\nL5: \nL6: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\nL7: \nL8: Description:\nL9: \nL10: [Button: Submit without GitHub] [Button: Submit in GitHub]\nL11: \nL12: cite0†Back to arXiv L13: \nL14: cite1†Why HTML?†info.arxiv.org cite2†Report Issue cite3†Back to Abstract cite4†Download PDF L15:   1. cite5†Abstract L16:   2. cite6†1 Introduction L17:   3. cite7†2 Related Work L18:     1. cite8†2.1 Non-Parametric Memory for LLMs L19:     2. cite9†2.2 Episodic Memory and Reasoning L20:   4. cite10†3 Methodology L21:     1. cite11†3.1 Indexing L22:     2. cite12†3.2 Agentic Inference L23:   5. cite13†4 Experimental Setup L24:     1. cite14†4.1 Datasets L25:     2. cite15†4.2 Methods for Comparison L26:     3. cite16†4.3 Metrics L27:     4. cite17†4.4 Implementation Details L28:   6. cite18†5 Results L29:     1. cite19†5.1 Episodic Recollection L30:     2. cite20†5.2 Episodic Reasoning L31:   7. cite21†6 Discussions L32:     1. cite22†6.1 Ablation Study L33:     2. cite23†6.2 Refusal Performance L34:     3. cite24†6.3 Human Evaluation L35:     4. cite25†6.4 Error Analysis L36:     5. cite26†6.5 Comparative Analysis: REMem vs. RAG L37:   8. cite27†7 Conclusion L38:   9. cite28†References L39:   10. cite29†A The Use of Large Language Models L40:   11. cite30†B Formal Definition L41:     1. cite31†Node L42:     2. cite32†Edge L43:     3. cite33†Index L44:     4. cite34†Algorithm L45:   12. cite35†C Detailed Results L46:     1. cite36†C.1 LoCoMo L47:     2. cite37†C.2 REALTALK L48:     3. cite38†C.3 Complex-TR L49:     4. cite39†C.4 Test of Time (Semantic) L50:     5. cite40†C.5 Semantic Memory Capability L51:     6. cite41†C.6 Ablation Study L52:     7. cite42†C.7 Performance by Temporal Category L53:   13. cite43†D Prompts L54:   14. cite44†E Implementation Details L55:   15. cite45†F Analysis L56:     1. cite46†F.1 Comparative Analysis: REMem vs. TISER L57:     2. cite47†F.2 Time and Space Efficiency L58:     3. cite48†F.3 Token Usage L59:     4. cite49†F.4 Example of the Extraction L60:     5. cite50†F.5 Graph Property L61: cite51†License: CC BY 4.0†info.arxiv.org L62: \nL63: arXiv:2602.13530v1 [cs.AI] 13 Feb 2026\nL64: # REMem: Reasoning with Episodic Memory\nL65: in Language Agents\nL66: Yiheng Shu    Saisri Padmaja Jonnalagedda Affiliation: The Ohio State University  Intuit AI Research{shu.251, su.809}@osu.edu, {saisri_jonnalagedda, xiang_gao}@intuit.com    Xiang Gao Affiliation: The Ohio State University  Intuit AI Research{shu.251, su.809}@osu.edu, {saisri_jonnalagedda, xiang_gao}@intuit.com    Bernal Jiménez Gutiérrez    Weijian Qi    Kamalika Das Affiliation: The Ohio State University  Intuit AI Research{shu.251, su.809}@osu.edu, {saisri_jonnalagedda, xiang_gao}@intuit.com    Huan Sun    Yu Su\nL67: ###### Abstract\nL68: Humans excel at remembering concrete experiences along spatiotemporal contexts and performing reasoning across those events, i.e., the capacity for episodic memory. In contrast, memory in language agents remains mainly semantic, and current agents are not yet capable of effectively recollecting and reasoning over interaction histories.\nL69: We identify and formalize the core challenges of episodic recollection and reasoning from this gap, and observe that existing work often overlooks episodicity, lacks explicit event modeling, or overemphasizes simple retrieval rather than complex reasoning. We present REMem, a two-phase framework for constructing and reasoning with episodic memory: 1) indexing, where REMem converts experiences into a hybrid memory graph that flexibly links time-aware gists and facts.\nL70: 2) agentic inference, where REMem employs an agentic retriever with carefully curated tools for iterative retrieval over the memory graph. Comprehensive evaluation across four episodic memory benchmarks shows that REMem substantially outperforms state-of-the-art memory systems such as Mem0 and HippoRAG 2, showing $3.4\\%$ and $13.4\\%$ absolute improvements on episodic recollection and reasoning tasks, respectively.\nL71: Moreover, REMem also demonstrates more robust refusal behavior for unanswerable questions.^{1}^{1} 1 Codes and data will be available soon.\nL72: ## 1 Introduction\nL73: The ability to intentionally and precisely re-experience events from our past is one of the defining features of human intelligence. This “mental time travel” capacity, or episodic memory, allows us to access specific events along a spatiotemporal axis, specifying their order, duration, and even causality.\nL74: Unlike semantic memory, which stores concepts and knowledge about the world, episodic memory shapes each individual’s unique experiences and preferences, establishing a cornerstone for continual learning within one’s environment.\nL75: Despite its importance for human cognition and rising interest in memory systems for language agents, achieving human-level episodic memory remains elusive, mainly due to the dominance of semantic memory paradigms in current approaches. Parametric memory, embedded in model weights during pre-training or fine-tuning, lacks adaptability and contextual grounding in specific experiences.\nL76: Model editing methods (cite52†Yao et al., 2023b ) can update stored facts but remain limited to modifying static semantic knowledge. Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) systems with embedding models (cite53†Zhang et al., 2025 ; cite54†Lee et al., 2025a ) enable dynamic knowledge access but still operate in a de-contextualized manner, divorced from spatiotemporal context.\nL77: Finally, more advanced non-parametric systems, which use large language models (LLMs) to construct summaries or semantic graphs (cite55†Gutierrez et al., 2024 ; cite56†Gutiérrez et al., 2025 ; cite57†Edge et al., 2024 ), improve over RAG but still prioritize structured world knowledge over lived, interaction-specific experience.\nL78: Recently, a few studies have targeted episodic settings more directly. These typically represent episodic memory using entity relationships in (temporal) knowledge graphs with a loss of coherent event contexts (cite58†Rasmussen et al., 2025 ) or by selectively inferring what seems important and offering it as a summary (cite59†Chhikara et al., 2025 ; cite60†Tan et al., 2025 ), which lacks explicit event modeling.\nL79: Critically, they fail to integrate situational dimensions, such as time, location, and participants, within interaction histories. Furthermore, when memory must support reasoning across multiple linked events, existing methods rely heavily on similarity-based retrieval, offering limited capacity to infer complex inter-event relationships. Given these observations, we believe that a comprehensive event representation, combined with flexible retrieval and reasoning, deserves further exploration.\nL80: cite61†Image: Refer to caption Figure 1: Overview of evaluation on episodic memory. Utterances are grounded to a timeline (top). We evaluate two progressive capabilities and show average scores on each (bottom): 1) Episodic recollection: recollect temporal and other situational elements of past experiences, measured by LLM-as-a-judge scores on LoCoMo and REALTALK.\nL81: 2) Episodic reasoning: multi-step reasoning across the timeline based on recollection, e.g., event-to-event relations, counting, and ordinal queries, measured by LLM-as-a-judge score on Complex-TR and the EM score on Test of Time.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCharacter-aware Transformers Learn an Irregular Morphological Pattern Yet None Generalize Like Humans (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.14100v1)\nciteturn31897view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2602.14100v1\",\"lineno\":65}); Total lines: 311\nL44:       2. cite34†4.2.2 Paradigm shape: models vs. humans L45:   6. cite35†5 Discussion L46:     1. cite36†Positional encoding and tag representation (RQ1). L47:     2. cite37†Structural pattern acquisition (RQ2). L48:     3. cite38†Productive generalization and comparison with humans (RQ3). L49:   7. cite39†6 Conclusion L50:   8. cite40†7 Limitations L51:   9. cite41†8 Bibliographical References L52:   10. cite42†‣ Character-aware Transformers Learn an Irregular Morphological Pattern Yet None Generalize Like Humans L53: cite43†License: CC BY-SA 4.0†info.arxiv.org L54: \nL55: arXiv:2602.14100v1 [cs.CL] 15 Feb 2026\nL56: # Character-aware Transformers Learn an Irregular Morphological Pattern Yet None Generalize Like Humans\nL57: ###### Abstract\nL58: Whether neural networks can serve as cognitive models of morphological learning remains an open question. Recent work has shown that encoder-decoder models can acquire irregular patterns, but evidence that they generalize these patterns like humans is mixed.\nL59: We investigate this using the Spanish L-shaped morphome, where only the first-person singular indicative (e.g., pongo ‘I put’) shares its stem with all subjunctive forms (e.g., ponga, pongas) despite lacking apparent phonological, semantic, or syntactic motivation. We compare five encoder-decoder transformers varying along two dimensions: sequential vs. position-invariant positional encoding, and atomic vs. decomposed tag representations.\nL60: Positional encoding proves decisive: position-invariant models recover the correct L-shaped paradigm clustering even when L-shaped verbs are scarce in training, whereas sequential positional encoding models only partially capture the pattern. Yet none of the models productively generalize this pattern to novel forms.\nL61: Position-invariant models generalize the L-shaped stem across subjunctive cells but fail to extend it to the first-person singular indicative, producing a mood-based generalization rather than the L-shaped morphomic pattern. Humans do the opposite, generalizing preferentially to the first-person singular indicative over subjunctive forms. None of the models reproduce the human pattern, highlighting the gap between statistical pattern reproduction and morphological abstraction.\nL62: Keywords: Cognitive Modeling, Morphological Inflection, Morphomes, Transformers, Positional Encoding\nL63: Akhilesh Kakolu Ramarao^{1}, Kevin Tang^{1,3}, Dinah Baer-Henney^{2}\nL64: ^{1}Department of English Language and Linguistics, Heinrich Heine University Düsseldorf\nL65: ^{2}Institut für Germanistik, Philologische Fakultät, Ruhr-Universität Bochum\nL66: ^{3}Department of Linguistics, College of Liberal Arts and Sciences, University of Florida\nL67: {akhilesh.kakolu.ramarao, kevin.tang}@uni-duesseldorf.de, dinah.baer-henney@rub.de\nL68: \nL69: Abstract content\nL70: ## 1.  Introduction\nL71: Inflectional paradigms are not merely lists of forms but constitute a central organizing principle of morphological knowledge (cite44†Stump, 2001 ). This claim, that the paradigm rather than the morpheme is the primary object of morphological analysis (cite45†Matthews, 1972 ), has profound implications for how we understand the mental lexicon.\nL72: If paradigms are cognitively primitive, then speakers do not assemble inflected words by concatenating stored morphemes; instead, they exploit implicational relations among whole forms, inferring unattested forms from partial paradigmatic knowledge (cite46†Ackerman et al., 2009 ).\nL73: Yet how far does this organization extend to irregular verbs? Can paradigmatic patterns that lack phonological, semantic, or syntactic motivation be learned and productively generalized? In this paper, we use computational modeling to probe these questions, training neural network models that differ in architectural choices on inflectional paradigms, comparing how these choices affect pattern acquisition, and evaluating their generalization behavior against human speakers tested on the same items.^{1}^{1} 1 Code and Data: cite47†https://anonymous.4open.science/r/code_cmcl2026/†anonymous.4open.science L74: We focus on morphomic patterns (cite48†Spencer and Aronoff, 1994 ), which provide the strongest test of paradigmatic learning because they lack any apparent phonological, semantic, or syntactic motivation. They challenge theories that require all morphological structure to be grounded in meaning or function, representing instead autonomous organization within the morphological system itself.\nL75: Such patterns are well attested across Romance languages (cite49†Maiden, 2011 ; cite50†Maiden, 2018 ; cite51†Maiden, 2021 ), making them ideal candidates for investigating whether computational models can acquire purely morphological generalizations.\nL76: In Spanish, the L-shaped morphome is one such pattern: the first-person singular indicative (pongo) shares its stem with every subjunctive form (ponga, pongas, …), while all other indicative forms use the regular stem (pones, pone, …). Table cite52†1 illustrates this pattern for the verb poner ‘to put’. The defining property is the grouping of 1 sg.ind patterns with all subjunctive forms rather than with its own mood category.\nL77:  | Indicative  | Subjunctive\nL78: 1 sg  | pong-o  | pong-a\nL79: 2 sg  | pon-es  | pong-as\nL80: 3 sg  | pon-e  | pong-a\nL81: 1 pl  | pon-emos  | pong-amos\nL82: 2 pl  | pon-éis  | pong-áis\nL83: 3 pl  | pon-en  | pong-an\nL84: Table 1: Present tense paradigm of poner ‘to put’. Hyphens mark stem-suffix boundaries. Bold marks the L-shaped stem.\nL85: Whether morphomic patterns constitute a psychological reality for speakers remains contested. cite53†Nevins et al. (2015) argue against the cognitive status of the L-shaped morphome with evidence from wug-test experiments on Spanish, Italian, and Portuguese, but subsequent work challenges this view. cite54†Cappellaro et al. (2024) provide experimental evidence that Italian speakers exhibit sensitivity to L-shaped patterns in processing and production tasks.\nL86: Similarly, cite55†O’Neill (2025) offer corpus-based evidence that gaps in Portuguese defective verbs cluster specifically in L-pattern cells rather than being randomly distributed across the paradigm. Computational modeling offers a complementary approach to this debate. cite56†Kakolu Ramarao et al. (2025) train neural sequence-to-sequence models on Spanish verbal inflection, providing form-morphological feature pairs as input and generating inflected forms as output.\nL87: They demonstrate that these models can reproduce L-shaped stem alternations, with frequency of L-shaped verbs playing a crucial role. However, their study employed a single architecture. We extend this line of work by comparing five model architectures to ask whether design choices affect the acquisition of morphomic patterns, and then evaluating their generalization behavior against human experimental data.\nL88: We focus on two architectural dimensions that could plausibly affect the acquisition of morphomic patterns: sequential vs. position-invariant positional encoding, and atomic vs. decomposed morphosyntactic tag representations.\nL89: Character-level encoder-decoder transformers have become the dominant approach for morphological inflection (cite57†Wu et al., 2021 ), but the Unimorph schema (cite58†Sylak-Glassman, 2016 ), which provides feature specifications for inflectional morphology, treats morphosyntactic features as unordered bundles: V;IND;PRS;1;SG and V;PRS;IND;SG;1 specify the same cell. This suggests that a tag should carry the same meaning regardless of its position in the sequence.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFrom Fluent to Verifiable: Claim-Level Auditability forDeep Research Agents (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.13855v1)\nciteturn31897view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2602.13855v1\",\"lineno\":140}); Total lines: 329\nL133: Current research agents provide no reconstructible trace linking generated claims to supporting evidence through explicit reasoning steps (cite50†Souza et al., 2025 ). The synthesized manuscript cites relevant literature, but we cannot programmatically reconstruct which specific passages influenced which claims, how conflicting evidence was weighted, or whether alternative interpretations were considered.\nL134: The inference chain exists only as transient activation patterns in the LLM’s forward pass that vanish after token generation (cite76†Lipton, 2018 ).\nL135: Why unverifiable inference chains prevent scientific verification: Popper’s criterion (cite83†Popper, 1959 ) for scientific claims requires that one should be able to systematically test assertions against evidence and identify contradictions.\nL136: This demands – (a) Identifying which specific evidence supports each claim, (b) Verifying that the evidence actually entails the claim (cite78†Metropolitansky and Larson, 2025 ), (c) Determining whether contradictory evidence was considered and properly addressed (cite84†Mantravadi et al., 2025 ), and (d) Assessing whether alternative interpretations were evaluated (cite85†Farquhar et al., 2024 ).\nL137: Current architectures make these steps intractable without complete manual re-analysis (cite65†Fernsel et al., 2024 ). When independent evaluators identified the energy efficiency paradox, they had to manually re-read the agent’s code, trace through execution logs, and reconstruct what the agent might have been optimizing for, requiring hours of expert time comparable to conducting the original research manually (cite42†Joeran Beel, 2025 ). This defeats the purpose of automation.\nL138: Research on auditability in AI systems emphasizes that verification effort must be substantially lower than generation effort for autonomous systems to provide practical value (cite65†Fernsel et al., 2024 ; cite64†Liao and Vaughan, 2024 ).\nL139: The architectural deficit is the absence of provenance graphs linking claims to evidence with explicit reasoning edges (cite50†Souza et al., 2025 ; cite52†Moreau and Groth, 2013 ).\nL140: The W3C PROV standard provides a foundation for this through entities, activities, and agents connected via fundamental provenance relationships (cite52†Moreau and Groth, 2013 ; cite28†Belhajjame et al., 2013 ), enabling structured queries like “which sources support claim C?” and “how was contradictory evidence from source S resolved?” Yet current research agents, including the AI Scientist, do not implement these standards (cite50†Souza et al., 2025 ).\nL141: Without such infrastructure, generated research remains scientifically unfalsifiable: we can test final claims experimentally, but cannot audit the reasoning that produced them. An initial step toward this is discussed in (cite86†Sung et al., 2025 ), where the authors introduce VeriLA, a systematic framework for evaluating agent failures that reduces human effort while improving interpretability.\nL142: VeriLA uses a human-aligned verifier module, trained on gold-standard human annotations, to assess each agent output, enabling fine-grained analysis, surfacing failures relative to human standards, providing actionable feedback, and lowering the cognitive burden on evaluators.\nL143: ### 3.4. Model dependent failures\nL144: The choice of foundation model including the scale and the weight accessibility also heavily determine the architectural failure modes introduced in the previous sections. Identical plan-execute-synthesize architectures exhibit qualitatively different failure patterns when instantiated using small open weight models vis-à-vis large proprietary systems (cite87†Zhou et al., 2024 ; cite61†Zhu et al., 2025b ).\nL145: Existing research agents treat the underlying language model as a black box and assume that increased scale suffices for autonomous research (cite37†Lu et al., 2024 ). However empirical evaluations show that model choice shifts failure location rather than reducing failure incidence. Smaller models fail during planning due to insufficient decomposition while larger models fail during synthesis through confident hallucination.\nL146: While increased scale alters, it does not eliminate these architectural limitations (cite61†Zhu et al., 2025b ; cite88†Chern et al., 2024 ).\nL147: The parameter capacity paradox: Open-source LLMs in the 7B–13B range generally lag behind commercial models on agent-style tasks, with prior benchmarks reporting substantially higher hallucination on factual question answering and weaker reliability in multi-step settings (cite87†Zhou et al., 2024 ; cite88†Chern et al., 2024 ).\nL148: Prior work attributes much of this gap to capacity limits; smaller models have weaker reasoning and working memory, which makes it harder to sustain consistent beliefs across long agent dialogues and increases the chance of fabricated intermediate steps (cite87†Zhou et al., 2024 ). Model scaling reduces these errors, but size alone is not decisive.\nL149: Across benchmarks, improvements within a model family from scaling can be large, yet cross-family comparisons suggest that architecture, data, and instruction tuning can matter as much as raw parameter count (cite88†Chern et al., 2024 ).\nL150: Moreover, end-to-end evaluations that require reproducing machine learning papers from scratch show that even strong proprietary models and research-agent systems frequently fail to deliver complete, correct results, with common breakdowns in experimental design, methodological rigor, and execution reliability (cite61†Zhu et al., 2025b ).\nL151: Smaller models often fail in the planning module because they cannot keep many constraints in mind, so they break a complex goal into overly simple sub-tasks and miss key requirements (cite87†Zhou et al., 2024 ; cite89†Lu et al., 2025 ). Larger models make fewer planning mistakes, but they can still produce a clean and confident plan that is wrong and looks correct at first glance (cite89†Lu et al., 2025 ). In the execution module, research agents run for a long time (about 46,000 seconds per task vs.\nL152: 250 seconds for reasoning-only agents), so early constraints get lost during coding and debugging (cite61†Zhu et al., 2025b ). This leads to “constraint drop” bugs, such as forgetting the intended cross-validation setup, and smaller models degrade more under long runs due to limited context (cite59†Liu et al., 2024 ; cite87†Zhou et al., 2024 ).\nL153: In the synthesis module, scale does not prevent hallucinations: reviewers can accept plausible but wrong abstracts, and even frontier models still hallucinate on technical QA, especially on multi-hop or conflicting evidence (cite90†Zheng et al., 2024 ; cite88†Chern et al., 2024 ; cite91†Min et al., 2023 ).\nL154: The open weight verification tradeoff: Closed-weight proprietary models (e.g., GPT-4, Claude 3.5 Sonnet) often outperform open-weight models but their closed design prevents inspection or modification of the reasoning process, which makes auditing, verification, and reproducibility difficult for autonomous research. In contrast, open-weight models can be fine-tuned to become competitive in low-data, low-resource, domain-specific settings, while remaining easier to adapt and control.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMAS-on-the-Fly: Dynamic Adaptation of LLM-based Multi-Agent Systems at Test Time (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.13671v1)\nciteturn31897view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2602.13671v1\",\"lineno\":174}); Total lines: 494\nL156: While the OP provides a collaboration blueprint, the inherent complexity of MAS often leads to unexpected anomalies during execution, such as agent malfunction, tool misuse, or shifts in the external environment cite55†Chen et al. (2023) .\nL157: Analogous to how biological systems employ homeostatic regulation to maintain stability amidst internal and external fluctuations, MASFly designs an experience-guided process supervision mechanism, where a Watcher refers to a personalized experience pool, monitors and optimizes agent behavior in real-time, thus achieving adaptive and reliable execution.\nL158: #### Personalized Experience Pool\nL159: Effective supervision requires the ability to identify and handle typical anomalies. To this end, we construct a Personalized Experience Pool (PEP) through a reflection mechanism by an LLM (detailed in Section cite15†3.4 ), which stores common error patterns and their corresponding remedies, supporting the supervision module continuously refine agents behaviors.\nL160: As shown in Figure cite81†4 , the each record is formalized as $<Q_{i},F_{i},P_{i}>$, which comprises the User Query $Q_{i}$, the Failure Cause $F_{i}$ (e.g., task misallocation, tool misuse), and the set of Agent-Wise Experiences $P_{i}=\\{p_{1},p_{2},…,p_{m}\\}$, where each $p_{k}$ encapsulates an Error Attribution diagnosing the agent’s specific contribution to the failure and an Improvement Strategy proposing corrective insights to prevent future recurrence.\nL161: By organizing agent-level failure cases and improvement strategies from past executions, the PEP serves as a knowledge base for adaptive monitoring and real-time behavioral refinement.\nL162: cite82†Image: Refer to caption Figure 4: The case illustration of the Personalized Experience Pool.\nL163: #### Process Supervision and Optimization\nL164: \nL165: During system execution, the supervision is carried out by a global Watcher Agent, which oversees system behavior, referencing both the OP and PEP. By retrieving relevant failure cases (indexed by the same queries as the SOP repository), the Watcher gains priors about potential pitfalls and uses them to implement dynamic, experience-informed interventions. Specifically, the Watcher conducts dual-level supervision:\nL166: \nL167:   * •\nL168: Inter-Agent Communication: verifying whether agents follow the prescribed workflow in the OP, maintain output consistency, and avoid unproductive conversational loops;\nL169: \nL170:   * •\nL171: \nL172: Agent–Environment Interaction: assessing whether agents properly invoke designated tools, respond appropriately to dynamic contexts, and prevent repetitive or irrelevant outputs.\nL173: \nL174: When an anomaly is detected, the Watcher can take either of two possible actions for optimization:\nL175: \nL176:   * •\nL177: Experience-Augmented Guidance: sending a feedback message to the faulty agent, incorporating insights from the PEP to enhance the agent’s reasoning or operational strategy in real time.\nL178: \nL179:   * •\nL180: \nL181: Dynamic Agent Replacement: removing the critically flawed agent and dynamically instantiates a new one $A_{i}$ to resume the task, ensuring workflow continuity.\nL182: To balance controlibity and efficiency, MASFly supports flexible intervention policies, such as triggering supervision every $M$ communication rounds, every $L$ environment-interaction steps, or capping the total number of interventions. Related experiments are provided in Appendix cite41†C.1 . Through this experience-guided supervision mechanism, MASFly enhances its ability to detect and address execution deviations, enabling dynamic adaptation throughout the entire execution process.\nL183: Table 1: Comparison of different methods.\nL184: Method  | Adaptive Prompts  | Adaptive Topology  | Adaptive System Generation  | Adaptive Execution  | Cross-Task Memory\nL185: MetaGPT  | $\\times$  | $\\times$  | $\\times$  | $\\times$  | $\\times$\nL186: AgentVerse  | ✓  | $\\times$  | $\\times$  | ✓  | $\\times$\nL187: MegaAgent  | ✓  | $\\times$  | $\\times$  | $\\times$  | $\\times$\nL188: GPTSwarm  | $\\times$  | $\\times$  | $\\times$  | $\\times$  | $\\times$\nL189: G-Designer  | $\\times$  | ✓  | $\\times$  | $\\times$  | $\\times$\nL190: AgentSquare  | $\\times$  | $\\times$  | $\\times$  | $\\times$  | ✓\nL191: MASFly  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓\nL192: Table 2: Performance comparison on TravelPlanner.\nL193: Mode  | Method  | Delivery Rate  | Commonsense  | Hard Constraint  | Final Pass Rate\nL194: Micro  | Macro  | Micro  | Macro\nL195: Two- Stage  | ReAct  | 90.6  | 60.4  | 7.2  | 24.0  | 16.6  | 5.6\nL196: Reflecxion  | 89.4  | 62.8  | 7.2  | 26.5  | 17.2  | 8.9\nL197: MetaGPT  | 95.6  | 65.3  | 14.4  | 19.8  | 16.1  | 8.9\nL198: AgentVerse  | 100.0  | 66.7  | 4.8  | 15.2  | 10.6  | 2.2\nL199: AgentSquare  | 98.3  | 81.2  | 33.3  | 20.5  | 15.6  | 13.3\nL200: MASFly  | 95.6  | 85.3  | 47.8  | 49.5  | 44.4  | 39.4\nL201: Sole- Planning  | CoT  | 100.0  | 75.8  | 13.3  | 55.2  | 48.9  | 6.7\nL202: MetaGPT  | 98.3  | 82.5  | 22.2  | 60.5  | 50.5  | 13.3\nL203: EvoAgent  | 95.6  | 69.2  | 13.9  | 59.5  | 47.8  | 8.3\nL204: AgentVerse  | 100.0  | 79.2  | 11.1  | 59.0  | 53.3  | 6.7\nL205: MegaAgent  | 94.4  | 82.9  | 26.2  | 60.5  | 46.9  | 10.0\nL206: SwarmAgentic  | 100.0  | 92.9  | 56.1  | 66.7  | 52.8  | 32.2\nL207: MASFly  | 98.9  | 92.9  | 68.3  | 68.3  | 65.6  | 61.7\nL208: ### 3.4 Reflective Experience Distillation\nL209: Upon task completion, MASFly conducts a reflection process guided by evaluation outcomes (training set only). For successful executions, the LLM distills the generated OP into a generalized and reusable SOP, which is stored in the SOP repository. For failed executions, MASFly prompts the LLM to diagnose shortcomings in the OP and attributing errors to specific agents.\nL210: These reflective insights serve two purposes: iteratively refining the instantiated SOP for successful task completion, and enriching the PEP to form agent-level priors that guide the process supervision during future executions. In this way, MASFly progressively strengthens both its system-generation capability and its robustness during execution, leading to increasingly reliable multi-agent coordination over time. More details are in Appendix cite32†B.1 .\nL211: ### 3.5 Comparison with Existing Works\nL212: We compare MASFly with representative methods that do not require additional LLM training in Table cite83†1 . As shown, MASFly uniquely enables adaptive generation of entire query-dependent systems. This is achieved through the retrieval-augmented SOP instantiation mechanism, where both agent prompts and communication topology are dynamically customized. It also supports adaptive execution by incorporating a process supervision and optimization mechanism.\nL213: Moreover, MASFly features an inherent memory mechanism that enables cross-task experience augmentation by reusing successful system-level experience from the SOP repository and agent-level experience of failure from the PEP. More discussions are in Appendix cite30†A .\nL214: ## 4 Experiment\nL215: Table 3: Performance comparison across 4 benchmarks.\nL216: Method  | GAIA  | HumanEval  | MBPP  | MBPP Pro\nL217: Level 1  | Level 2  | Level 3  | Avg.\nL218: Base  | 29.3  | 11.3  | 0.0  | 16.4  | 94.94  | 91.49  | 82.80\nL219: ReAct  | 32.9  | 28.3  | 6.3  | 26.8  | 95.57  | 91.79  | 82.97\nL220: MetaGPT  | 34.1  | 32.1  | 6.3  | 29.1  | 96.20  | 90.67  | 82.20\nL221: AgentVerse  | 30.5  | 19.8  | 6.3  | 20.9  | 96.84  | 90.91  | 81.65\nL222: MegaAgent  | 32.9  | 26.4  | 3.1  | 25.5  | 97.15  | 92.38  | 83.11\n"
